  SETS  hair keep 417897  body keep 64456  face keep 20560  legs keep 26936
  GRID body 1182 cells, 64456 verts, mean 54.5, max 2660, p90 105
  GRID face 48 cells, 20560 verts, mean 428.3, max 1820, p90 1530
  CELL 0.05  REACH 0.05  cells scanned per query 27

=== 3. Query counters ==========================================================
Same clip, averaged over the first two MEASURED frames (penetration runs on
every second frame, so a measured frame is two simulated ones).

  QUERY per measured frame: calls 1183481  mapGets 31953987  nonEmpty 9415692
        (29.5%)  vertsScanned 1504276479  vertsPerCall 1271.1  returnedREACH 27.0%

  31953987 / 1183481 = 27.00 exactly, which is the (2r+1)^3 = 27 cells a query
  scanned. 9415692 / 31953987 = 29.47%, so 70.5% of the map reads hit nothing.
  1504276479 / 1183481 = 1271.06.

  calls 1183481 is NOT 417897 x 2. The hair is queried against the body grid and
  the face grid, and the skirt is queried against a third, the leg grid. The
  three together make up the count.

=== 4. Old and new implementations, side by side ===============================
Both classes built on the same sets, both run on every query the simulation
issued, results compared with !==.

  AB over 2366962 queries: mismatches 0  worst |delta| 0
  TIME old 10.2s   new 2.0s   speedup 5.2x

  2366962 / 1183481 = 2.0 exactly: this covers the first TWO measured frames of
  411, about 0.5% of the clip. It is an exhaustive comparison over those frames,
  not a sample of the whole clip.

  5.2 is the ratio of the unrounded totals. The two printed figures are rounded
  to one decimal and 10.2 / 2.0 gives 5.1; the smaller number is the one the
  rounding moves. Both sides carry the same two performance.now() calls per
  query, so the overhead is in both.

